ARTICLE_ROWS='''tax-tips/amend-return/|Before you amend a tax return.|Start with what changed, which year it affects, and the documents behind it.|Find the original~Keep the return actually filed with its supporting records. A working draft can differ from the submitted version.^Describe the change~Write down the item you believe needs correcting and why. Keep corrected forms with your records.^Review the instructions~The IRS explains amended returns and status tracking. Discuss the change before assuming an amendment is needed, including any related state filings.|https://www.irs.gov/filing/amended-return-frequently-asked-questions
tax-tips/family/7-requirements|Getting ready to discuss child-related tax benefits.|Organize household information for a careful eligibility review.|Build a timeline~Record household changes and relevant living arrangements. Use dates rather than relying on memory.^Gather information~Keep identity, relationship, residence, and support information that may be relevant. Keep private details out of general inquiries.^Check current rules~Eligibility depends on the benefit and tax year. Consult current IRS guidance and discuss your circumstances with a qualified preparer.|https://www.irs.gov/credits-deductions/individuals/child-tax-credit
tax-tips/family/guide-to|Questions about head of household status.|Prepare the facts before deciding which filing status fits.|Record circumstances~Note marital status, household arrangements, and changes during the year.^Organize costs~Keep records of the costs you paid to maintain the home. Identify amounts paid by others.^Check requirements~Head of household has specific conditions. Review IRS Publication 501 and discuss unclear circumstances.|https://www.irs.gov/publications/p501
tax-tips/family/rules-for|Preparing for questions about dependents.|A household overview makes a dependent-related conversation easier.|List relevant people~Record relationships and living arrangements. Keep sensitive identity information outside general inquiries.^Keep support records~Gather records explaining household support and changes in who paid for what.^Resolve shared questions~When more than one person may be involved in claiming a dependent, check the applicable rules before filing.|https://www.irs.gov/publications/p501
tax-tips/general/|Keep tax changes in context.|Check the year, the official guidance, and your circumstances.|Identify the year~Announcements may discuss a different year from your return. Read effective dates carefully.^Use primary information~Start with IRS guidance and the relevant instructions. A headline is not enough to explain a change.^Make a question list~Record the changes that may relate to your work, household, or business and bring supporting facts to the discussion.|https://www.irs.gov/newsroom
tax-tips/investments-and-taxes/|Get your investment sale records ready.|Organize the facts behind a sale before discussing its reporting.|Collect transaction records~Gather sale confirmations, purchase records, and brokerage statements. Note missing or corrected information.^Check transferred holdings~Retain earlier records when assets move between accounts. The latest statement may not tell the whole story.^Discuss the treatment~Timing and the investment type can affect treatment. Use official guidance rather than assuming every sale is treated alike.|https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc409
tax-tips/irs-tax-return/can-i-file|Do you need to file with little or no income?|Begin with your circumstances, not just one income figure.|Take an inventory~List the income documents, payments, and other information you received. A missing paycheck does not describe the entire year.^Review requirements~Filing requirements depend on the year and circumstances, including income types and filing status.^Consider your questions~If tax was withheld or another issue exists, discuss whether filing may be appropriate. Low income alone does not settle every question.|https://www.irs.gov/individuals/check-if-you-need-to-file-a-tax-return
tax-tips/irs-tax-return/current|A clearer way to think about tax brackets.|Separate total income, taxable income, and rates.|Start with the right amount~Income and taxable income are different. An illustrative tool should state which it uses and its deduction assumptions.^Understand the layers~Federal brackets apply progressively. Reaching a higher bracket does not make its rate apply to all income.^Use an estimate appropriately~Our 2025 tool illustrates ordinary-income calculations. Credits, other taxes, and individual circumstances can change a filed result.|https://www.irs.gov/filing/federal-income-tax-rates-and-brackets
tax-tips/irs-tax-return/how-do-i-file|Organize a prior-year filing plan.|Work through past years with separate records and a missing-item list.|Separate years~Make one folder per year for income, payments, correspondence, and return copies.^Rebuild the record~Look for missing statements and consider official transcripts. Track information still needed.^Agree on priorities~Explain the whole picture when several years are involved. Review the correct forms and requirements for each year.|https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/filing-past-due-tax-returns
tax-tips/irs-tax-return/how-to-find|Finding your prior-year AGI.|Locate the submitted return or an official record.|Use the filed version~Look for the return actually submitted, keeping it separate from drafts.^Find the relevant line~Use that year's instructions to identify adjusted gross income. Do not substitute wages or a refund amount.^Use official help~If a copy is unavailable or electronic filing instructions are unclear, consult IRS guidance on prior-year information and transcripts.|https://www.irs.gov/individuals/electronic-filing-pin-request
tax-tips/marriage/|Questions to discuss when filing as a couple.|Bring both sides of the financial picture to the conversation.|Organize both sets~Collect income, payments, and relevant records for each spouse. List the states and changes involved.^Compare the whole situation~A filing-status choice can involve more than one number. Ask how your options would be reviewed.^Document the decision~Confirm the approach and responsibilities before filing. Check the official rules for the year involved.|https://www.irs.gov/publications/p501
tax-tips/self-employment-taxes/|Make sense of contractor income records.|Bring payment records and income forms together.|Collect every source~Gather client payments, platform reports, invoices, and information returns. Keep income records even when no form was received.^Reconcile overlaps~A payment may appear in multiple reports. Compare them with your own records instead of adding every form together.^Organize expenses~Group receipts and note the business purpose. Prepare a summary, then discuss the tax treatment separately.|https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/self-employed-individuals-tax-center
tax-tips/tax-planning-and-checklists/|Build a tax calendar for your situation.|Use the year, return type, and official deadlines.|List the filings~Personal, business, federal, and state obligations can follow different schedules.^Verify current dates~Use IRS and state agency resources. The year, location, and special relief can affect dates.^Plan ahead~Allow time to gather records, resolve questions, review, and confirm submission. Keep evidence of filings and payments.|https://www.irs.gov/filing/individuals/when-to-file
tax-tips/unemployment/|Organize unemployment benefit records.|Bring statements, withholding information, and other income together.|Gather the statement~Locate the benefit statement for the relevant year and compare it with payment records.^Note withholding~Keep records of tax withheld and other income received during the year.^Resolve discrepancies~If a statement differs from your records, contact the issuing agency through its official channel. Review IRS guidance for related tax questions.|https://www.irs.gov/individuals/employees/unemployment-compensation
'''
for row in ARTICLE_ROWS.strip().splitlines():
 prefix,t,i,topics,src=row.split('|');matches=[r.removesuffix('/index.html') for r in ROUTES if r.startswith(prefix)];assert len(matches)==1,(prefix,matches);add(matches[0],t,i,topics,'article','Tax guides',src)
